      "widgets_values": [
        "# Pulse Voice · lip sync that says where it starts\n\n**One `LoadAudio`, one `PulseVoice`, every window.** Before this node the only\nroute to `lip_sync` was a shot's own `ref_audio`, so a narration covering the\nwhole film needed one wire per shot — four identical wires to one file on a\nfour-window film. `voices.voice_1` on Pulse Slate is visible to every window.\n\n## The thing that used to go wrong silently\n\nA `lip_sync` clip is **cut to the window it rides in**. That makes *which seconds\nof the file* the whole question, and until `aligns_to` existed nothing in the\ngraph could answer it — the trim assumed the recording began with the film and\nsaid so nowhere.\n\n- **`film_clock`** (set here) — the recording's first sample is the film's second\n  zero. This is what a single narration track covering the whole timeline does.\n- **`shot_start`** — the recording's first sample is the start of the shot it is\n  wired to. This is what a take recorded for one shot does. Wire the PulseVoice\n  into that shot's `voice` input rather than into the slate.\n\nGet it wrong and the window is handed **silence**: no error, no failed render,\njust a mouth that does not move. The report now says so, in seconds.\n\n`offset_seconds` is added on top of whichever base you pick, for a take with a\ncouple of seconds of room tone in front of it.\n\n## The placeholder is deliberately too short\n\n`example_pulse_voice.wav` is a **one-second** test tone and the film is 13.88s,\nso the first thing this graph tells you is:\n\n> `@Voice` covers 0.00-1.00s on the film clock and window 1 covers 0.00-6.58s,\n> so it runs out 5.58s before it ends. That much of the window has no recording\n> behind it, and the shots there still carry written dialogue — so the model\n> speaks those words while the supplied track is silent, and the mouth moves over\n> dead air.\n\nThat message **is** the feature. Point `LoadAudio` at your own narration — aim\nfor **13.9s or a little more** — and it goes away.\n\nThe two shots add up to 13.17s but the render is 13.88s: a window is a whole\nnumber of frames on H3's 17k+5 grid, so the last one is stretched to the nearest\nlegal count rather than cut short. The report says so under `duration snapped to\nthe frame grid`, and the recording has to cover the *rendered* length, not the\nrequested one.\n\n## Why the shot durations look odd\n\n`6.5833` is 158 frames, and 158 is on H3's 17k+5 frame grid. On-grid durations\nput every cut exactly on a window seam, so no shot is compiled into two windows\nand no window is handed two copies of the same voice. `partition_strategy` is\n`shot_aligned`, which reports whether it managed it.\n\n## What to check before spending a render\n\n- **`compiled_prompt`** (bottom left) — `subject_definitions` must carry\n  ``Their lip movements match `<Audio 1>` precisely``, and `retention_analysis`\n  must carry ``` `<Audio 1>`: fully_copy ```. Those two sentences *are* the\n  mechanism: the text encoder never hears the waveform, so wiring the socket\n  tells the model nothing on its own.\n- **`report`** (bottom right) — the lip-sync notes, the window table, and which\n  cuts `shot_aligned` reached.\n\n## Two speakers\n\nGive each their own `PulseVoice`, set `speaker` to their `@Name` in the Asset\nBin, and put each on the shot they speak in. A window carrying two lip-sync\nreferences is reported: both are cut to the same seconds, so the mouth has more\nthan one answer.\n\n`use_reference_audio` is **on**, so your take is muxed into the finished film\ninstead of H3's re-synthesis of it. Every voice in a window is mixed, not just\nthe first.\n"
      ]
